{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 390,
    "dDurationMs": 5710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Space is big, but it’s peanuts compared to the eternally inflating multiverse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6100,
    "dDurationMs": 6860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But just how many bubble universes does the eternally inflating multiverse contain?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12960,
    "dDurationMs": 1370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What are they like?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14330,
    "dDurationMs": 4410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And most importantly, what can they tell us\nabout aliens?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24220,
    "dDurationMs": 6299,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Imagine it: the observable part of our universe\nis 93 billion light years across, and that’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30519,
    "dDurationMs": 4141,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just a small fraction of the stuff created\nin our Big Bang."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34660,
    "dDurationMs": 7770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in the eternal inflation picture, ours\nis just one among uncountable bubble universes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42430,
    "dDurationMs": 6170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bubbles that are continuously appearing and\ngrowing within a vastly larger spacetime that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48600,
    "dDurationMs": 4250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "itself expands at an exponentially accelerating\nrate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52850,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A greater inflationary spacetime whose expansion\nnever ends."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58250,
    "dDurationMs": 6259,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We looked at the bizarre idea of eternal inflation\nin recent episodes – but we stopped short"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64509,
    "dDurationMs": 4051,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of exploring the full implications of this\nproposition."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68560,
    "dDurationMs": 4230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those implications are, frankly, completely\nnuts."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72790,
    "dDurationMs": 1760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some may also be true."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74550,
    "dDurationMs": 4810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here’s the scenario: the default state of\nthe greater universe – or multiverse - is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79360,
    "dDurationMs": 6100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to expand exponentially due to the vacuum\nof space itself having a large and constant"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85460,
    "dDurationMs": 1769,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy density."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87229,
    "dDurationMs": 4181,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Energy locked into something called the inflaton\nfield."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91410,
    "dDurationMs": 5499,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Within that inflating space, tiny patches\nstop inflating – the inflaton field in that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96909,
    "dDurationMs": 5301,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "patch loses its energy and so accelerating\nexpansion stops there."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102210,
    "dDurationMs": 6460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Within each bubble we get a new Big Bang that\nkicks off a more slowly expanding universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108670,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that bubble has an edge, and the edge\nspreads into the surrounding inflating spacetime"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113810,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at the speed of light, causing inflation to\nstop within the growing bubble."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118790,
    "dDurationMs": 4610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If lots of these bubbles form, they could\ncollide with each other to produce a connected"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123400,
    "dDurationMs": 3130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "network of non-inflating universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126530,
    "dDurationMs": 5750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the little patches where inflation persists\nare expanding so fast that they quickly dominate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132280,
    "dDurationMs": 6630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the non-inflating network of bubbles, and\nso the cycle of inflation continues."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138910,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some questions spring to mind: - I mean, besides\n“What?!?”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142950,
    "dDurationMs": 3690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, how many bubble universes can\nbe made this way?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146640,
    "dDurationMs": 1560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How often do they collide?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148200,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Are those universes similar to each other,\nor wildly different?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151860,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’ll give you an idea of our best thinking\nfor all of these."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155440,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s start with the first two, because\nthese are basically our challenge question."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161920,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To fully answer these we’d need to know\nthe true physics of the inflaton field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166540,
    "dDurationMs": 5900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But we can figure some stuff out just based\non the exponential nature of the expansion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172440,
    "dDurationMs": 5769,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, in the challenge question I asked\nyou to calculate the RELATIVE number of new"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178209,
    "dDurationMs": 6831,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bubble universes that formed in one second\ncompared to the previous second."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185040,
    "dDurationMs": 5369,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We assumed that there’s a fixed but unknown\nprobability that a bubble universe will form"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190409,
    "dDurationMs": 3041,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in any given volume of inflating space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193450,
    "dDurationMs": 4230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means the number of bubbles forming at\neach point in time should be proportional"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197680,
    "dDurationMs": 3710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the volume of the inflating universe at\nthat time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201390,
    "dDurationMs": 5890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the rate at which that volume is increasing\nis the same as the rate at which bubble universes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207280,
    "dDurationMs": 1179,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "multiply."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208459,
    "dDurationMs": 5841,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We assumed the minimum inflation rate that\nwe think was needed to kick off our own universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214300,
    "dDurationMs": 6439,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The scale factor – or radius of the universe\n– increased by a factor of at least 10^26"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220739,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in less than 10^-32 seconds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224959,
    "dDurationMs": 5271,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So how much did the volume increase over 1\nfull second at this rate?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230230,
    "dDurationMs": 6530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, a scale factor or radius increase of\n10^26 means a volume increase of that cubed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236760,
    "dDurationMs": 3220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "– so 10^78."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 239980,
    "dDurationMs": 8729,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Every 10^-32 seconds one cubic meter of volume\nbecomes 10^78 cubic meters, which is approximately"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248709,
    "dDurationMs": 3051,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the volume of our entire universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251760,
    "dDurationMs": 7820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And then in the next 10^-32 seconds each of\nthose 10^78 new 1-meter cubes spawns just"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 259580,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as many new entire universe-sized volumes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263020,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we have 10^78-squared, then 10^78-cubed,\netc."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265360,
    "dDurationMs": 10690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the time we get to one full second we’ve\nmultiplied by 10^78 10^32 times, so our volume"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276050,
    "dDurationMs": 8930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is 10^78 to the power of 10^32, or around\n10^10^34."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284980,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And our number of new bubble universes should\nmultiply by the same insane factor each second."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291740,
    "dDurationMs": 5510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This gives us a ridiculously large number\nof universes even if the probability of making"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297250,
    "dDurationMs": 3970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one per unit of volume is insanely low."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301220,
    "dDurationMs": 3940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The exponential nature of the expansion guarantees\nthat."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305160,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As soon as the inflating spacetime is big\nenough to make one universe, in the next second"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 310200,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it should make 10^10^34 universes, and so\non."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 315750,
    "dDurationMs": 2310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you got this right then congrats."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318060,
    "dDurationMs": 7640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll list the winners as well as answer\nthe extra credit question at the end of this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325700,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "video."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 326700,
    "dDurationMs": 5730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As crazy as eternal inflation sounds, it’s\nstill fun to think about the consequences"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 332430,
    "dDurationMs": 1520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if all of this is true."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333950,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here’s another one: do all of these bubble\nuniverses the same laws of physics, or could"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340270,
    "dDurationMs": 2990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they be wildly different from each other?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343260,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Based on standard ideas, they probably have\nthe same number of dimensions as ours – 3"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347940,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space, 1 time – but their contents and physics\ncould be very different."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353120,
    "dDurationMs": 6190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example the cosmological constant – the\nstrength of dark energy – could be different."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359310,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Remember that it was the high energy density\nof the inflaton field that drove inflation,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365490,
    "dDurationMs": 3820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the loss of that energy density that ended\nit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 369310,
    "dDurationMs": 5830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what if the inflaton field retained just\na tiny bit of energy after its decay?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375140,
    "dDurationMs": 3890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That residual field might be what we observe\nas dark energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379030,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s an open and contentious question why\nthe current energy density of the vacuum should"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385210,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be so low without being exactly zero given\nthat it started out so large."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 391730,
    "dDurationMs": 4770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Seems awfully lucky – if dark energy were\nmuch stronger then our universe would have"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396500,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "restarted its accelerating expansion too quickly\nfor galaxies and stars and life to ever form."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403100,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here’s a possible explanation: what different\nbubble universes can end up with different"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 409540,
    "dDurationMs": 1900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vacuum energies?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411440,
    "dDurationMs": 6100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Very low vacuum energies like ours might be\nextremely rare, but there are so many bubble"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417540,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universes that at least some will have a low\nenough cosmological constant for life to form."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422840,
    "dDurationMs": 3290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Naturally, we would be in such a universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426130,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is an example of using the Anthropic\nPrinciple - we must exist in a universe capable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431440,
    "dDurationMs": 6110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of producing us, so if there are many universes\nit is natural that we find ourselves in one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437550,
    "dDurationMs": 2750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "finely tuned for life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 440300,
    "dDurationMs": 6090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "People have also invoked the anthropic principle\nplus eternal inflation to explain a conundrum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446390,
    "dDurationMs": 1530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in string theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 447920,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As we’ve talked about before, modern string\ntheory is difficult to pin down because there"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 452920,
    "dDurationMs": 6030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are countless possible minimum-energy configurations\nof its 6 curled up dimensions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458950,
    "dDurationMs": 7480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "More than 10^500 possible vacuum states – probably\na lot more."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 466430,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The vast space of possible configurations\nof these compact dimensions is referred to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 472070,
    "dDurationMs": 2680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as the string landscape."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 474750,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Each different configuration results in a\ndifferent family of particles and also a different"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479430,
    "dDurationMs": 2150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cosmological constant."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 481580,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We don’t know why our universe has the particular\nstring vacuum state that it does."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 487300,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it’s lucky it does – because the resulting\nparticles allow for things like complex chemistry."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494500,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Enter the anthropic principle once again:\neternal inflation gives us enough universes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500080,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to easily populate the entire string landscape."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 504270,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All different vacuum states exist, and our\nuniverse necessarily has one that leads to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511170,
    "dDurationMs": 1820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "life-friendly particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 512990,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As well as a decent cosmological constant."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516170,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some people aren’t comfortable with the\nanthropic principle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519890,
    "dDurationMs": 3970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those people will be even less comfortable\nwith the next idea."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523860,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This one is from Alan Guth, the guy who invented\ninflation in the first place."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529030,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here it is: eternal inflation may solve the\nFermi Paradox."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534290,
    "dDurationMs": 4330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It may explain why we don’t see aliens."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538620,
    "dDurationMs": 7340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The argument goes like this: if the number\nof new universes increases by a factor of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545960,
    "dDurationMs": 6290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some impossibly large number every second,\nthen the vast, vast majority universes in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 552250,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "existence were born recently."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556210,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are vastly more universes born one second\nafter ours than were born at the same time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 563010,
    "dDurationMs": 1360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as ours."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564370,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, imagine that there’s a set amount of\ntime for the first intelligent life to form"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 570370,
    "dDurationMs": 1700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in any one of these universes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 572070,
    "dDurationMs": 2710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s say 10 billion years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574780,
    "dDurationMs": 3090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Pretend that it’s the same in all universes\ndown to the second."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 577870,
    "dDurationMs": 3990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The real timescale is going to be much more\nfuzzy than this, but it also doesn’t really"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581860,
    "dDurationMs": 2380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter for this argument."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 584240,
    "dDurationMs": 6410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, the first intelligence appears in our\nuniverse after exactly 10 billion years – we’ll"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 590650,
    "dDurationMs": 3610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "call that moment “second one”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 594260,
    "dDurationMs": 5390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The same happens in the exact same second\nfor universes that formed at the same time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 599650,
    "dDurationMs": 2070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as ours."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601720,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One second later – “second two” – you\nmight get some more intelligent lifeforms"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 607020,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "forming in those same universes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609720,
    "dDurationMs": 2870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Maybe just as many as in second one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 612590,
    "dDurationMs": 6230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But consider the wave of universes that formed\none second after our own."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 618820,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These are only now reaching the 10 billion\nyear mark and producing their first intelligent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 625060,
    "dDurationMs": 1300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lifeforms."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626360,
    "dDurationMs": 5970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And of course there are vastly more of those\nslightly younger universes – more than all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 632330,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the atoms in all the universes that are one\nsecond older."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 637640,
    "dDurationMs": 6250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So first civilizations in those younger universes\nwill always outnumber all the civilizations"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 643890,
    "dDurationMs": 1940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in all older universes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645830,
    "dDurationMs": 5910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The result is that at any one moment, almost\nall of the intelligent beings in the eternally"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 651740,
    "dDurationMs": 6750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inflating multiverse are in the youngest universes\nthat have had time to form intelligent life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658490,
    "dDurationMs": 5670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if we imagine that we are a typical intelligent\nlifeform, then we’re most likely the most"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664160,
    "dDurationMs": 4930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "common type across the multiverse, which means\nwe’re the first to appear in our universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 669090,
    "dDurationMs": 4300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ergo we shouldn’t be surprised that we don’t\nsee aliens."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 673390,
    "dDurationMs": 3980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Guth calls this argument the Youngness Paradox."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677370,
    "dDurationMs": 4550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s be clear – he doesn’t necessarily\nbelieve it to be right."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 681920,
    "dDurationMs": 6070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He admits that, while it seems logically sound,\nit does seem weird enough to force us to question"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687990,
    "dDurationMs": 1560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the premises."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689550,
    "dDurationMs": 5570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He proposes that there may be something off\nin the logic of weighing up probabilities"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695120,
    "dDurationMs": 1930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over bubble universes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 697050,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He also doesn’t rule it out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700930,
    "dDurationMs": 4390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, the last thing I want to talk about is\ncolliding universes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705320,
    "dDurationMs": 1600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How often does it happen?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706920,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s start by answering the extra credit\nquestion: how close to bubbles need to be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712500,
    "dDurationMs": 1520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in order to collide?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 714020,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I won’t go through the detailed calculation\nbecause it takes a wee bit of calculus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719040,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Instead I’ll provide a link to the answer\nin the description."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 722480,
    "dDurationMs": 6110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the too-long-didn’t-calculate is this:\nremember that bubble edges expand at the speed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 728590,
    "dDurationMs": 1180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729770,
    "dDurationMs": 5870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If they form too far apart then the intervening\ninflating spacetime will throw them apart"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735640,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at faster than light speed before they can\nmerge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 739160,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Assuming the crazy rate of inflation in the\nquestion, you get that two bubble edges need"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744120,
    "dDurationMs": 7250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be closer than 6x10^-50 m in order to reach\neach other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 751370,
    "dDurationMs": 4540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is something like 15 orders of magnitude\nsmaller than the Planck length."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 755910,
    "dDurationMs": 4970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The implication is that universes don’t\ncollide very often unless their rate of production"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 760880,
    "dDurationMs": 1800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is extremely high."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 762680,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it might be very high – especially if\nthe inflaton field is highly correlated from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767430,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one point to the next, as in slow-roll inflation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 771620,
    "dDurationMs": 5610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it still should NOT be surprising that\nwe don’t see evidence of bubble collisions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 777230,
    "dDurationMs": 2450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in our observable universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779680,
    "dDurationMs": 4050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If such collisions happened, they’re probably\ntoo far away for us to see."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 783730,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so we’re come up with a lot of ridiculously\nlarge AND small numbers, and convincingly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790170,
    "dDurationMs": 2680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shown that none of this is easily testable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 792850,
    "dDurationMs": 2980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Where does that leave eternal inflation?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795830,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well we probably shouldn’t just accept it\n– but at the same time, current lack of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 801710,
    "dDurationMs": 2590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "proof doesn’t mean something is wrong."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 804300,
    "dDurationMs": 4550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just that the universe – or multiverse is\nplaying hard to get."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808850,
    "dDurationMs": 5010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But here’s something that’s not hard to\nget – Space Time swag."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813860,
    "dDurationMs": 4550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If your name is listed below, you’re a winner\nof the challenge question and you get your"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818410,
    "dDurationMs": 3130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pick of a swag item from our merch store."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 821540,
    "dDurationMs": 5710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Email us your choice, any relevant size info,\nand your mailing address and we’ll get it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 827250,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out to you."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828250,
    "dDurationMs": 5750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Of course you can reward yourself for just\nlistening to the answer by buying your own"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834000,
    "dDurationMs": 1060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "swag."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 835060,
    "dDurationMs": 1150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Link in the description."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836210,
    "dDurationMs": 4230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s the best humorous science apparel in\nall of spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 840440,
    "dDurationMs": 2750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Did you get enough spacetime today?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 843190,
    "dDurationMs": 1090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "No?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 844280,
    "dDurationMs": 4790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Need more of a fix of that existenial awe\nat the wonder and weirdness of the universe?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 849070,
    "dDurationMs": 3140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Got burning question about the nature of reality?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852210,
    "dDurationMs": 2170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I may have what you need."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854380,
    "dDurationMs": 6980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We just launched a spacetime discord for 24-7\nconversations on all of the above."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 861360,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's for our patreon members - but don't worry\n- it's the lowest of the lowest tiers - 2"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 867020,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bucks a month to help support the show and\nto indulge that voracious curiosity of yours."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873500,
    "dDurationMs": 2080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hope to see you over there."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 875580,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And one the subject of Patreon, I wanted to\ngive a big, big thanks to Alexander Tamas,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 880800,
    "dDurationMs": 2470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one o our big bang supporters."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883270,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alexander has been traveling the bubble universes\nfor many years, supporting local youtube space"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888020,
    "dDurationMs": 1970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shows where he finds them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889990,
    "dDurationMs": 6310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alexander, we're glad you found us, and safe\ntravels, wherever they may lead."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 896300,
    "dDurationMs": 4450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Last time we visited that old argument - Is\nPluto a planet?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 900750,
    "dDurationMs": 5430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The question gets to the heart of what a scientific\ndefinition really is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 906180,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And a number of you found the International\nAstronomy Union's new defition of planet - the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 911480,
    "dDurationMs": 3990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one that excluded Pluto - not very scientific\nat all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 915470,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I thought it worth focusing on that for this\ncomment response."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 919850,
    "dDurationMs": 4970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A number of people in the comment section\nechoed these thoughts - but special props"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 924820,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to Regolith on the spacetime discord, who's\na bona fide planetary scientist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 930080,
    "dDurationMs": 4990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I'm also echoing some of the thoughts of Alan\nStern, lead scientist on the new horizons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935070,
    "dDurationMs": 2390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mission to pluto."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 937460,
    "dDurationMs": 7570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are two main arguments - firstly that\nthe IAU definition is arbitrary and ambiguous,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 945030,
    "dDurationMs": 7050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "second that it should have been planetary\nscientists that made this decision, not astronomers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 952080,
    "dDurationMs": 6100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I acknowledged the arbitrariness in the episode\n- but ambiguity is a more serious concern."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 958180,
    "dDurationMs": 3349,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that's something a scientific definition should\nnever be."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 961529,
    "dDurationMs": 7851,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here the IAU definition again: \"A planet is\na sun-orbiting body massive enough to be round"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 969380,
    "dDurationMs": 3730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and to have cleared it's orbit of debris\"."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 973110,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's that last bit - cleared its orbit - that\nexcludes Pluto."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 976750,
    "dDurationMs": 5350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This might be considered arbitrary because\n\"clearing the orbit\" doesn't necessarily reflect"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 982100,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a fundamental difference in the physical nature\nand formation process of the body."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 987680,
    "dDurationMs": 3090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that's an issue that planetary scientists\ntake."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 990770,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, whether or not a body clears its\norbit depends strongly on where in the solar"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 996150,
    "dDurationMs": 6030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "system it forms - the further out, the more\nmassive a body needs to be to clear its orbit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1002180,
    "dDurationMs": 4590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If Earth formed at 100 times its current distance\nfrom the sun it wouldn't have cleared its"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1006770,
    "dDurationMs": 2440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "orbit either, and so wouldn't be considered\na planet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1009210,
    "dDurationMs": 5830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But Jupiter in the same location would have."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1015040,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we end up with this somewhat complicated\nrelationship between mass and distance from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1019920,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Sun that determines whether you get called\na planet - and that relationship may not relate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1025020,
    "dDurationMs": 3950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to any scientifically interesting properties\nof the body."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1028970,
    "dDurationMs": 2709,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So there's your arbitrariness."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1031679,
    "dDurationMs": 1280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what about ambiguity?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1032959,
    "dDurationMs": 6061,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Within our solar system it's clear enough\nwhich are planets and which aren't by this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1039020,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "definition."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1040020,
    "dDurationMs": 2789,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what about around other stars?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1042809,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That brings us to the first part of the IAU\ndefinition - a planet has to be orbiting our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1048339,
    "dDurationMs": 1201,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1049540,
    "dDurationMs": 4420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's right, other stars don't have planets\n- they have exoplanets."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1053960,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The word planet is reserved exclusively for\nthe 8 bodies of our solar system."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1058100,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact you need this combination of requirements\nto know absolutely whether something is a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1063420,
    "dDurationMs": 1850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "planet by the new definition."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1065270,
    "dDurationMs": 5770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In general, the new definitely of planet avoids\nambiguity but at the cost of arbitrariness"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1071040,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- and you might also say of scientific usefulness."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1075480,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Pluto aside, the new definition allows for\ntwo objects with extremely similar geophysical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1081010,
    "dDurationMs": 3090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "properties to be differently classed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1084100,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For now the new definition makes things clearer\nthan they were, but as our understanding grows"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1091000,
    "dDurationMs": 5490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- particularly of exoplanets, maybe we need\nto be ready to change the definition again"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1096490,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- perhaps this time with more input from planetary\nscientists."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1100890,
    "dDurationMs": 22490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Who, after all, are THE literal experts and\nmore frequent users of the term."
    } ]
  } ]
}
